# React Native and Expo dependencies

- Add or update native-facing packages with `npx expo install <pkg>` (never plain `npm install` for them), so the version matches the installed Expo SDK.
- `react`, `react-dom`, `react-native`, `react-native-reanimated`, `react-native-worklets`, `react-native-gesture-handler`, `react-native-screens`, `react-native-safe-area-context`, and every `expo-*` package are pinned by the SDK. Never bump one alone because `npm outdated` shows a newer major; they move together in an SDK upgrade (`expo-upgrade` skill).
- Keep the project aligned within its SDK: `npx expo install --check` and `npx expo-doctor` report nothing before a release. Fix with `npx expo install --fix` in its own commit, then rebuild the native app (a JS reload is not enough).
- Don't add a state, data, form, list, or image library (TanStack Query, Zustand, MMKV, react-hook-form, Zod, FlashList, expo-image, keyboard-controller) as a side effect of a task. Use what the project has; propose an addition separately with the reason.
- Prefer packages that support the New Architecture and the installed React Native version; check the package's compatibility notes before installing.
- Replace abandoned packages with known advisories (for example the npm `xlsx` 0.18.x line — use SheetJS's own distribution) when they parse user-supplied files.
- Lockfile changes ship in the same commit as `package.json`.
